  "deliveryRule": "UNSETTLED for a busy target. Mid-turn delivery acked once (run A: BUSY-ACK back at 12.9s, 2.7s before the turn ended) and was not reproduced afterwards; the runs that showed failure were invalid (an ack matcher using \\b against JSON-escaped text, and a worker that backgrounded its sleep so there was no busy window). No valid run has shown a busy target failing to receive. relay therefore treats a busy target conservatively: hold the socket send until the turn boundary. That is safe either way — a delay if delivery works, required if it does not. Idle and stopped targets are settled: idle takes the socket, stopped takes `claude --bg --resume <uuid> \"<msg>\"`.",
  "deliveryBusyOverSocket": "unsettled — observed working once, never observed failing under a valid busy window, not reproduced",
  "deliveryBurst": "10/10 arrived, each exactly once, to an idle session — no dedupe needed and no loss",
  "socketOutcomeSignal": "There is no status frame, so a send is `unknown` until a marker is seen. `UserPromptSubmit` is NOT sufficient: it fires only for a message that STARTS a turn, so a frame merged into a running turn is delivered and acted on while firing no hook at all (burst: 10 replies returned, 2 and 8 hooks across two runs). Evidence ladder: the `[relay #<id>]` marker in `UserPromptSubmit`, else the marker in the worker's reply frame on relay's own inbox socket, else the marker in the transcript tail. Without the last rung a delivered mid-turn send stays `unknown` and blocks its task queue.",
